-Definition 2.1 fixes identifiability as uniqueness of a sum of k simple
-tensors.  Lemma 3.2 places eight general Segre points on a (3,1,1)
-Segre-Veronese fourfold, Lemma 3.3 uses an ancillary Macaulay2 calculation to
-show that its tangential projection has a six-point reduced fibre, and Theorem
-3.5 concludes that a general rank-eight tensor in C^3 tensor C^6 tensor C^6 has
-at least six decompositions.  Remark 3.7 explicitly says the paper cannot prove
-that six is the exact number.  Corollary 3.8 restates the phenomenon as multiple
-rank-one spanning sets for a matrix space.
-
-The Introduction also identifies the regimes that do not support a hardness
-claim: Kruskal-type bounds make sufficiently small general rank identifiable,
-and the quoted BCO Corollary 8.4 completely characterizes identifiability in
-the unbalanced range.  More decisively for this generated task, an exact
-dependence among displayed simple tensors is always obtainable by flattening
-and nullspace elimination.  That polynomial algorithm rules out Track A and is
-measured explicitly below as the Track B reference algorithm.
-
-That theorem is existential and does not hand a generator alternate
-decompositions.  Merely summing eight sampled simple tensors would know only
-one witness.  This module instead composes a finite-difference identity.  At
-n=6 it takes sixteen synchronized polynomial-curve points in exactly the
-paper's tensor space.  Every coordinate has degree at most 14, so the signed
-15th-difference coefficients give an exact dependence.  Its two sign classes
-contain eight terms each.  Random unimodular changes of basis obscure the
-coordinate curves without changing the certificate; the certificate is never
-obtained by solving the constructed instance.
+Definition 2.1 defines k-identifiability as uniqueness of a sum of k simple
+tensors. Lemmas 3.2--3.3 use a special (3,1,1) Segre-Veronese fourfold and an
+ancillary Macaulay2 calculation; Theorem 3.5 then proves that a general
+rank-eight tensor in C^3 tensor C^6 tensor C^6 has at least six decompositions.
+Remark 3.7 says the exact number is unknown.
 
-The Step-0 algorithm warning makes this Track B.  Flattening the displayed
-simple tensors and computing a nullspace by modular Gaussian elimination is a
-polynomial O(n^4) algorithm and succeeds on every generated instance.  The
-module runs and measures it under reference_algorithm.  The compact route only
-sorts the integer parameters and generates one binomial row by its recurrence.
-Outlier height, one-moment greedy cancellation, bounded random restarts, and the
-obvious alternating-linear ansatz are all tested separately and fail.  The
-matrices are changes of basis, so canonical_key deliberately ignores them and
-keys the exponent configuration and scale-normalized parameter set.
+That proof is existential and does not output alternate decompositions for a
+scalable exact sampler. This module stays in the paper's simple-tensor language
+but composes a special identity. For P(z)=product_j(z^2-x_j^2), the functional
+sum_{P(t)=0} f(t)/P'(t) vanishes for deg(f)<=14. At d=6 all tensor-coordinate
+products have degrees 0,...,14, so normalized reciprocal derivatives give a
+dependence of sixteen simple tensors. Its two signs give two eight-term
+decompositions, and unimodular mode changes preserve the certificate.
 
-The attacks fail for checkable construction-specific reasons: random basis
-changes and column order sever factor height from coefficient rank; cancelling
-only the zeroth moment leaves higher moments nonzero; bounded restarts sample
-distinct coefficients without bias toward the one-dimensional nullspace; and
-alternating linear magnitudes do not satisfy the required high-degree moments.
+This is Track B: exact flattening plus rational Gaussian elimination is the
+known polynomial reference method. The compact route uses
+P'(x_j)=2*x_j*product_{k!=j}(x_j^2-x_k^2), one gcd, and a reorder. Increasing n
+raises node and coefficient bit size while the witness stays at sixteen unit
+fractions and the compact route stays below 300 operations. The construction
+is a rational special family: it does not claim genericity, minimal rank, or
+the paper's lower bound of six decompositions.
 """.strip()
 
 
